                             PA P E R T R A I L



   The same table, with the cardboard under the short leg. The same
window. My mother thirty-four years old, hair pulled back, a dishtowel
over her shoulder, standing, not sitting, with a boy of six asleep in the
next room. And across the kitchen, in the doorway, hat in hand: a man
in a gray coat with a small black notebook.
   “May I?” said Adrian Vale, gesturing at the chair.
   He was younger, his hair still dark. The voice was the same. Low, pa‐
tient, a carrier wave.
   “No,” said my mother.
   “Mrs.—”
   “No, gracias.”
   He didn’t argue. He smiled. He let a silence open, a long, soft,
weighted one, the kind you’d feel obligated to fill. My mother, in her
kitchen, in the quiet, felt it. I watched her feel it. Something in her
shoulders began to slide.
   I counted. I couldn’t help it. Six. Seven.
   And my mother, with the flat of her hand and not a flicker of hesita‐
tion, reached out and turned the radio on.
   Loud.
   A merengue, full brass, tambora, a singer who didn’t believe in sub‐
tlety. Her whole kitchen went up like a room on fire. She started the
pot. She banged a spoon. She asked him, in a voice over the music, if he
wanted to leave through the door or the window. The silence he’d been
building collapsed under a trumpet.
   He stood in the doorway for one more beat. He wrote something in
his notebook. Then he put on his hat and left, and I heard him go down
four flights of stairs with no hurry at all.
   The kitchen was only Sofía’s dining room again, the wall of string,
Doña Aurora’s saint.
   “¿Qué viste?” said my mother.
   I told her. All of it. The radio. She looked at me, and her face did a
thing I’d never seen it do, and then she folded her hands against her
chest.
   “I didn’t know about eight seconds,” she said. “I didn’t know any‐
thing. I knew he was waiting for me to fill his quiet.” Her voice was dry



